95:- **Its own threads.** `crates/semantic/src/checker.rs` creates a `tsrs_api::Session` on a thread of its own with a 512 MiB stack, the stack the `tsrs` binary serves on: a query runs on the calling thread, and a type 2,000 deep overflows a default 2 MiB thread, which aborts the process. Requests from any thread reach it over a channel and are answered one at a time; a whole-project `extract` starts a second thread with a channel (a lane) of its own and asks two programs over the same root files, each program's requests down its own lane (`tsrs_api` locks a program's one checker for the length of a request, so two programs are what can be answered at once). Each program has a snapshot of its own (`Rpc::open_programs`): `tsrs_api` registers the handles it hands out in one table per snapshot, behind one lock, which two programs of one snapshot waited on. The second snapshot is derived from the first (`updateSnapshot` with `createPrograms`), so it reads every file from the first's cache, as the first read it, and neither goes to the disk again nor parses a file again: a second `createSnapshot` repeated the file system work of a program build (stat, lstat and read of every file it resolves), which took 25 to 35 ms and raised the run's system CPU time. Every program holds every root file, so either answers a component as one program would; the Storybook session asks one; `Session::handle_request` is the dispatcher the `tsrs` binary serves, so `rpc.rs` sends the methods and JSON of `tsc --api`. A stack overflow aborts and cannot be caught (deep-type tests: 2,000 levels in every run, 10,000 in release).
96:- **Guards** (trail 369 slice 2). A panic inside the checker comes back as an error for that request (`tsrs_api` catches it, and the thread catches any that escapes), then every lane of the session stops (a whole-project run fails on the first lane's error and answers structurally, with one diagnostic) and the last to leave closes the session: a session a panic unwound through is not asked again, and in a Storybook session the next request starts a fresh one (three crashes and the checker stays down for the session). A panic `tsrs` reports as the client's fault (an unknown `moduleResolution`) is an error like any other and the session is kept. `tsrs` parses on threads of its own with 256 MiB stacks, which a file nested a few hundred thousand levels deep overflows (measured: an object type 200,000 deep parses, 300,000 aborts; generic, tuple, JSX and arrow chains abort between 300,000 and 1,000,000); so every file the checker reads from the disk is measured first, its deepest nesting of `(`, `[` and `{` and, apart, of `<` (5 ms for Chakra's 4.7 MB of declarations, whose deepest is 187), and a request that read one past 20,000 fails with a `SEMANTIC_BACKEND` warning naming it, its components keeping their structural answer. Requests that build snapshots, the ones that read files, run one at a time across the lanes, so a refusal is attributed to the request that met it. A snapshot the refused request made is released before the error goes back, as is one made for a caller that stopped waiting: its id never reaches anyone who could release it. Nesting written without brackets (a chain of arrow types, `a ? b : …`, `1 + 1 + …`) is not measured.
98:- **Failures.** A checker thread that cannot start, a request past its deadline and a stopped checker give one `SEMANTIC_BACKEND` warning.
102:The client sends `createPrograms[].compilerOptions` (the Node client sends `options`; the server accepts both, and `crates/semantic/tests/resolve.rs` pins the key). Every test that asks a checker (`resolve`, `tsrs_real`, `cases`, `families`, `declared`, `memo`, `exports`, `composes`, `instantiate`, `watch`, `on_demand`) asks the built-in one.
106:A long-lived session (the Storybook addon, the lazy `WatchSession`) attaches a warm checker instead of running the whole-project pass above (ADR 0022): a napi session does unless `checker: false`. `caliper-docgen-semantic` keeps one checker warm behind a worker thread, answers from a program rooted at only the requested component's file, and returns its props. The program's snapshot is kept between requests and brought up to date with the files the session says changed ("Watch", below). `WatchSession::component` merges them into the structural answer by name (the checker's type wins, a structural prop the checker did not return stays, and `htmlAttributes` applies once after the merge, as in the structural pass) when they arrive within `semanticWaitMs` (default 150 ms), or within `semanticWaitEmptyMs` (default 2,000 ms, never less than `semanticWaitMs`) when the structural answer lists no props at all (a `ComponentProps<typeof Button>` of a package the structural pass does not model, whose props type it could not expand); otherwise it returns the structural answer plus an `Info` diagnostic, and the checker's late answer serves the next request of the same component while no source file has changed. A kept answer carries what the checker said about the request that made it (a warning that it was not told about an edit, why a component kept its structural result), and every request it serves reports that again, a late answer's included; only an unavailable checker's warning is said once per session. There is one request in flight per component key and content hash of its file: a caller that asks while it is in flight (a story asked while its own prefetch runs, or two stories of one component) waits for that answer, so the checker answers it once. The checker answers interactive requests (`WatchSession::component`, `components`) before background ones (`components_in_background`, which `sessionPrefetch` and the addon's background prefetch of the next stories use, and which waits for nobody), first come first served within each; an interactive caller that joins a background request raises it, and after 4 interactive answers in a row while background requests wait, the oldest of them goes next. Any source-file change, including a file the component only imports, drops every checker answer, so the next request asks again; an answer still in flight at that moment is neither kept nor joined. The component's structural diagnostics that the checker's props supersede are dropped when they replace the structural ones, except while a structural prop the checker did not return is still opaque: those diagnostics name the component, not the prop, so they stay until nothing they could be about is left. (The whole-project merge drops unreturned structural props, so it has no such case.) Which ones are superseded is in "Diagnostics a checker answer supersedes".
108:Measured with `crates/semantic/examples/ondemand_bench.rs` (50 random components, TypeScript 7.1.0-beta): the first answer takes 82 to 137 ms (Chakra 703 ms), a component asked for alone 21 to 41 ms (Chakra 534 ms), and 2 to 9 ms when asked for in batches of 8 to 32. Where the structural pass is behind (shadcn) the checker reports 2.3 times the props; where it is not (Mantine, Chakra, Spectrum) it reports the same count. The in-memory cache, the 64-program replacement and the failure modes are in the ADR. (These numbers were measured over the `tsc --api` pipe, before the checker was linked in.)
113:- **Memory** (trail 369 slice 2): after the first request and every fourth, once the reply is sent, the worker reads what the checker holds and compares it with `OnDemandConfig::max_rss_mib` (768 MiB). The bound is between requests: one request that needs more finishes first. This process's resident memory is the host's as well (Node, Storybook, and Caliper's own answers, which grow by about 5.5 MiB per Chakra component asked), so what is read is the resident part of the one address range `tsrs` carves its arenas from (`mincore`, about 6 ms). Past the bound the checker stops for the session: every later request keeps the structural answer, with a `SEMANTIC_BACKEND` warning that names the bound. It is never replaced, because a fresh session does not lower what this process holds: a closed session gives back part of its arenas (Chakra's 46 MiB fell to 22 to 37), and the fresh one parses every file again. Measured on Chakra (`docs/gate-measurements.md`): 650 edits peak at 377 MiB never replaced against 514 MiB replaced every 64 programs, and the arenas stay at 46 MiB over 2,000 edits and reach 165 MiB over 937 Chakra and MUI components. The range is the process's, so it covers every program and lane of the session; the bound is applied by the Storybook session's warm checker (one lane), and stopping it stops all of it. A whole-project `extract` (two lanes) has no arena bound, since it ends with its process; it has a heap budget instead.
114:- **Heap budget** (trail 404): a whole-project run (`resolve`, `resolve_cached`, `resolve_early`, which `extract` and `check` call) reads what mimalloc has committed (`mi_process_info`, about 0.9 µs) before each component and every 32 steps, a step being a checker request or a type the converter converts or recalls from its memo. A memo hit copies its tree, and Chakra's own-source style system built 39 GB of those copies in the first five components without many requests, so reading only between components or requests is too late. The budget is 60% of the memory the process may use, at most 8 GiB (`HeapBudget::machine`): the physical memory, or on Linux the lowest memory limit of its cgroup and their ancestors (`/proc/self/cgroup`, v2 `memory.max`, v1 `memory.limit_in_bytes`) when lower, so a container or CI job with a limit gets a budget under it; there is no option for it. Past it the run takes no further step: the conversion in flight stops, every component not yet answered keeps its structural result, and one `SEMANTIC_BACKEND` warning names them and the heap it read. **Past the budget the output depends on timing** (how far each lane had got), as the warning says, and such an answer is not kept in the cache. Under it nothing changes. Measured peak commits with the budget at 8 GiB: Mantine core 1.1 GiB, shadcn 1.1 GiB, ant-design 0.96 GiB, react-spectrum 1.0 GiB, `gate-large` 1.1 GiB, `chakra-only` 0.96 GiB; Chakra's `packages/react/src` stops at 8.1 GiB (4.7 GiB with a 4 GiB budget) where it reached 42 GB. The arena bound above and the `OnDemand` session are unchanged: they do not run under the budget.
115:- **Converted on the checker's thread:** an answer is converted on the checker's own thread (`Client::run_on_thread`), in a runtime of its own there, so each of its requests (about 3,200 for Chakra's `Button`) is answered in place instead of crossing a channel to the checker and back. The guards are the same: a panic in the session ends it, each request is bounded by `request_timeout` (past it the answer stops waiting and the work's later requests fail, a release excepted), and a panic in the conversion itself leaves the session answering. The first answer for `ChakraButton` went from 145 to 115 ms, and one edit-and-query of `ButtonProps` (`watch`) from 152 to 119 ms at the median (load 6 to 9; these include the reference and prop-key allocation changes).
116:- **Start and stop:** creating the checker and `warm_up` return at once, and dropping the checker waits at most `shutdown_wait` (5 s) for a request in progress before letting the worker finish alone. The checker's thread finishes a request in progress, then closes its session and exits.
117:- **Warm-up snapshot:** `warm_up` also builds the snapshot the first request starts from: a program rooted at a file held in the checker's memory (never on disk) that imports each package the nearest `package.json` lists under `dependencies` and `peerDependencies` (`@types` packages are read anyway). Its files are stamped like a request's. The first request brings it up to date with `updateSnapshot`, re-rooted at its own files and with announced edits read again, instead of reading the lib and every dependency from the disk with `createSnapshot`; the warm snapshot is then released. An edit nobody announced is caught as for any kept snapshot. The memory bound is checked after the warm-up too. On `gate-large` (Chakra and MUI) the warm-up takes 63 to 77 ms, its arenas hold 38 MiB, and after the first answer they hold 49 to 50 MiB instead of 40 (peak RSS 20 to 35 MiB higher); `ChakraButton`'s first answer went from 146 to 156 ms to 126 to 149 ms (load 7 to 9).
119:- **Latency, re-measured** (`ondemand_bench`, 30 components, release build, `7.1.0-beta`): shadcn first answer 84 ms, median 21 ms, p90 37 ms; antd first 93 ms, median 36 ms, p90 40 ms. The memory check does not add to a story's wait.
142:- **Recursion** is a `recursive` marker named by the declaration (the alias or interface) with the instantiation's type arguments. Whether a type is one it is inside of is decided by its type handle, which is the instantiation: `Box<string>` inside `Box<Box<string>>` is another type from the one around it and is expanded, while `Tree<string>` inside `Tree<string>` is a back-reference; an alias of an array or tuple of itself (`type Loop = Loop[]`) ends at the first lap. A handle is only compared inside one conversion, never stored in an answer; type handles change with the order of queries, so a test asks last to first and compares bytes. The per-snapshot memo is keyed by the handle, the depth and the set of enclosing handles, so a hit gives what a miss would.
144:- **Debug stat.** With `RUST_LOG=caliper_docgen_semantic=debug`, each component logs its request count and milliseconds, and the session logs requests by method and memo hits.
211:- **An edit nobody announced.** Neither checker sees an edit it is not told about (an update without the file in `fileNotifications` answers from the old text, and so does one told about another file), so before an answer the kept snapshot is checked: after each reply the files its program read are listed (`getSourceFileNames`, 1,855 for Chakra's `Button`) and stamped by modification time and length, and before the next answer they are stamped again (about 1.5 ms on four threads). A file that changed and was not announced makes the answer come from a fresh snapshot (`createSnapshot` reads everything from the disk), with a `SEMANTIC_BACKEND` warning that names the file. A file modified within 2 s of the snapshot's read cannot be told from a later edit by its stamp, so it is read again at every update until it settles: never a warning, never stale. A file that cannot be listed or stamped makes the next request build afresh.
213:- **Memory.** Every request counts toward the 64 programs of a process, and the memory check is unchanged; a replaced process takes its snapshot with it. The built-in checker keeps one session and its snapshot for the whole watch session, bounded as under "On demand" above.
248:- **Measurement.** The construct suite's structural lane passes `--no-checker`: its expectations are the resolver's. `scripts/weekly.py` (its time, memory, recall and oracle runs), `scripts/agent_eval.py` and `just bench` run the default, the checker, and the weekly's per-corpus budgets are "record on first run" (`docs/measurement.md`, "The budgets"). The checker lane, which held the checker path before, now runs the default, the built-in checker.
